<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pearl River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zhujiang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pearl River is the great river system of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, formed by the West, North and East rivers, which meet in a broad delta at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> before reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>. In ancient times its western branches linked the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guizhou">Guizhou</a> with the lowlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a>. Its delta, which now includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a>, has been a centre of maritime trade since antiquity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pearl-river.jpg" alt="Pearl River" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tung Wu from Shenzhen, China · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Humen_Bridge-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Pearl River, in Chinese Zhujiang, is an extensive river system in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The name is also used for the watersheds of its tributaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, the Xi, Bei and Dong rivers, which all flow into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> through the Pearl River Delta. Measured from the furthest reaches of the Xi River, the system of the Pearl, Xi, Xun, Qian, Hongshui and Nanpan rivers is 2,400 kilometres long. It is China's third-longest river system after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, and its second largest by volume after the Yangtze.</p><p>The Pearl River Basin covers 453,700 square kilometres. It drains most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a>, which together are known as Liangguang, as well as parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guizhou">Guizhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a>, and the northernmost parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-bang">Cao Bằng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lang-son">Lạng Sơn</a> provinces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. The name Pearl River is also given to one branch within the delta, the widest distributary but a short one, which is first so called just north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>. The name comes from the pearl-coloured shells lying in the riverbed where it flows through Guangzhou.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Its mouth forms a large bay in the south-east of the delta, the Pearl River Estuary, where the Bocca Tigris separates Shiziyang in the north from Lingdingyang in the south, and Jiuzhouyang at the southern tip is surrounded by the Wanshan Archipelago. The bay separates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> and Zhuhai from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenzhen">Shenzhen</a>, and the Bocca Tigris is regularly dredged to keep it open to ocean vessels. Cities on the river system include Liuzhou, Guilin, Nanning, Wuzhou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhaoqing">Zhaoqing</a>, Foshan, Dongguan and Zhongshan, as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>, Hong Kong, Macau, Shenzhen and Zhuhai.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pearl River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In a sea battle off the <b>Pearl River</b> delta, Yuan forces crushed the Song navy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-19">19 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It controlled the Zangke River route down to the <b>Pearl River</b> and the lands of Nanyue.</blockquote><a href="/state/yelang">Yelang</a></li></ul></section>
